Dual-use disclosure for pi-cursor-reins
========================================

pi-cursor-reins is a provider plugin for the pi coding agent
(npm: @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent). It lets pi use models from the
user's own Cursor subscription. The following security-relevant
capabilities are inherent to that purpose. All of them are documented,
tested, and statically auditable in this repository (docs/SPEC.md,
docs/PROTOCOL.md, docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT.md, and `npm run audit`).

1. Host identity collection (src/identity)

   Cursor's API requires a stable client machine identifier. The
   provider reads platform machine ids (Linux /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
   and /etc/machine-id files; macOS ioreg IOPlatformUUID; Windows
   MachineGuid registry value; FreeBSD kenv/sysctl host UUID) through a
   fixed, documented command allowlist with array argv and no
   user-input concatenation, hashes them (SHA-256), and sends only the
   hash in request headers on the optional inference channel. The
   default agent channel sends no machine identity at all (Cursor CLI
   headers carry no checksum). The raw hardware id never leaves the
   process. Nothing else is collected. Linux reads the machine-id
   files directly without spawning a process. No fallback UUID is
   generated or written to disk.

2. Credential handling (src/auth, src/transport)

   OAuth login runs through pi's own `/login cursor` flow. Access and
   refresh tokens are stored only in pi's auth.json (pi AuthStorage),
   refreshed over HTTPS, and placed in request headers. Tokens never
   enter any subprocess environment (statically enforced by
   `npm run audit`). Credentials are transmitted only to
   api2.cursor.sh.

3. Network access (src/transport)

   HTTPS egress is limited to an audited domain allowlist:
   api2.cursor.sh (OAuth, model catalog, optional inference channel)
   and agentn.*.api5.cursor.sh (agent inference channel). The protocol
   definitions are original TypeScript rewritten from observed wire
   behavior; no Cursor or third-party code is copied.

4. Subprocess spawning (src/identity only)

   Only the identity commands listed in (1) may spawn, with array argv.
   The audit tool (tools/audit) fails if any other spawn/exec/execSync
   call site exists in the codebase.

The provider never executes model-requested local tools. All tool
execution stays inside pi under pi's permission system; Cursor-side
native tool execution requests are rejected by a completeness-tested
audit table (see README, "Why this exists", and docs/SPEC.md FR-2).
